{"name":"Prime Pink","hex":"#ff8d86","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"prime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["First in importance, degree, or rank.","First in time, order, or sequence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The first hour of daylight; the first canonical hour.","The religious service appointed to this hour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prime"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prime number","blocks":[{"text":"A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that is not a product of two smaller natural numbers. A natural number greater than 1 that is not prime is called a composite number. For example, 5 is prime because the only ways of writing it as a product, 1 × 5 or 5 × 1, involve 5 itself. However, 4 is composite because it is a product (2 × 2) in which both numbers are smaller than 4. Primes are central in number theory because of the fundamental theorem of arithmetic: every natural number greater than 1 is either a prime itself or can be factorized as a product of primes that is unique up to their order."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Primes-vs-composites.svg/330px-Primes-vs-composites.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number","wikidata":"Q49008"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Prime_number_Cuisenaire_rods_7.png/500px-Prime_number_Cuisenaire_rods_7.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Prime_number_Cuisenaire_rods_7.png/500px-Prime_number_Cuisenaire_rods_7.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Demonstration, with Cuisenaire rods, that 7 is prime, because none of 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6 divide it evenly","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrime_number_Cuisenaire_rods_7.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Rhind_Mathematical_Papyrus.jpg/500px-Rhind_Mathematical_Papyrus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Rhind_Mathematical_Papyrus.jpg/500px-Rhind_Mathematical_Papyrus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARhind_Mathematical_Papyrus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Prime_numbers_in_arithmetic_progression_mod_9_zoom_in.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Prime_numbers_in_arithmetic_progression_mod_9_zoom_in.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrime_numbers_in_arithmetic_progression_mod_9_zoom_in.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ulam_2.png/330px-Ulam_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ulam_2.png/330px-Ulam_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Ulam spiral. Prime numbers (orange) cluster on some diagonals and not others. Prime values of \n  \n    \n      \n        4\n        \n          n\n          \n            2\n          \n        \n        −\n        2\n        n\n        +\n        41\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle 4n^{2}-2n+41}\n  \n are shown in blue.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUlam_2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png/500px-Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png/500px-Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plot of the absolute values of the zeta function, showing some of its features","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARiemann_zeta_function_absolute_value.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Gears_large.jpg/330px-Gears_large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Gears_large.jpg/330px-Gears_large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The small gear in this piece of farm equipment has 13 teeth, a prime number, and the middle gear has 21, relatively prime to 13.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGears_large.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Sieve_of_Eratosthenes_animation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Sieve_of_Eratosthenes_animation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The sieve of Eratosthenes starts with all numbers unmarked (gray). It repeatedly finds the first unmarked number, marks it as prime (dark colors) and marks its square and all later multiples as composite (lighter colors). After marking the multiples of 2 (red), 3 (green), 5 (blue), and 7 (yellow), all primes up to the square root of the table size have been processed, and all remaining unmarked numbers (11, 13, etc.) are marked as primes (magenta).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASieve_of_Eratosthenes_animation.gif"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Pentagon_construct.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Pentagon_construct.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Construction of a regular pentagon using straightedge and compass. This is only possible because 5 is a Fermat prime.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APentagon_construct.gif"}],"sounds":{"prime":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-prime.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/3/39/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-prime.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-prime.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/3/39/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-prime.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-prime.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-prime2.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.or